---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import site from "../data/site.json";
import { features, providers } from "../lib/data";
---

<Base title="How we checked" description="Sources, confidence tags, dates, and the limits of this research.">
  <PageHeader
    kicker="Method"
    title="How we checked"
    standfirst="Every claim comes from a provider's own documentation, pricing page, or a named third party. Each carries a confidence tag, an exact quote and a link. Nothing is inferred into a fact, and nothing unpublished is estimated."
    meta={[`${providers.length} providers`, `${features.length} features`, `Checked ${site.checkedOn}`, "No sandbox run"]}
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="section section--flush">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What was done</h2>
        <p>
          For each provider, a researcher read the documentation, the pricing page, the FAQ, the
          terms and, where it existed, the machine-readable OpenAPI or documentation bundle. Each
          profile records the exact quote, its URL and a tag. The profiles live beside the site in
          <code>research/</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          No account was created, no sandbox was started and nothing was billed. Every statement
          about behaviour is a documentation claim, not a measurement. Where a provider's pages
          contradict each other, both statements are shown and the row is marked as a contradiction.
        </p>

        <h2>Confidence tags</h2>
      </div>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Tag</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {site.confidenceTags.map((tag) => (
            <tr><td><span class="tag tag--docs">{tag.tag}</span></td><td>{tag.meaning}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Dates</h2>
        <p>
          All provider profiles were checked on {site.checkedOn}. Prices changed during October 2026
          at several providers — boxd cut its rates by about 20% between 9 and 28 October, and Boat
          moved from credit packs to a dollar wallet — so treat any number as a dated observation,
          not a standing fact. Boat's documentation is served through a cache that can return an
          older revision, so its figures were re-fetched with cache-busting URLs.
        </p>

        <h2>Limits worth stating plainly</h2>
        <ul>
          {site.limits.map((limit) => <li>{limit}</li>)}
        </ul>

        <h2>About battleships.dev</h2>
        <p>
          battleships.dev is a comparison dataset built by the Boat team. We used it as a
          cross-check and never as a primary source. It disagreed with live provider pages often
          enough that its numbers are not repeated here as fact: it described InstaBox's pricing and
          sizes as they were before 20 October, boxd's pre-cut rates in one table row (its headline rate is current), and
          Runtime's idle default as it was before 18 October. Its own Boat record is dated
          30 September and disagrees with Boat's current docs on disk sizes and snapshot retention.
        </p>
        <p>
          That does not make it useless. It is a fast way to find which questions to ask, and the
          right place to price your own workload. It is a bad way to settle a claim about behaviour.
        </p>

        <h2>Open questions we could not settle</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Why Sandboxer's sizes, hourly rates and plan tiers match Boat's exactly while its disk sizes, regions, stopped-disk retention and legal entity differ. No public source explains the relationship.</li>
          <li>Whether Agent 37's Cloud dashboard can create a sandbox by clicking. No documented path exists, but the absence may be a documentation gap.</li>
          <li>Whether InstaBox's free plan requires a card. The pricing page says no; the API has a state that says yes.</li>
          <li>What a stored snapshot costs at Lizard, and what backup storage costs at boxd. Neither is published.</li>
          <li>Whether egress is free at Agent 37 and boxd. Neither publishes a rate or a cap; Boat says it does not bill egress.</li>
          <li>Morph's regions, maximum machine sizes and GPU availability. Its terms name the company (Morph Labs, Inc., San Francisco), but none of these is published, and two third-party sources disagree on whether it has raised funding.</li>
          <li>Whether Daytona's 10 GiB per-sandbox disk cap can be raised, and at what price. Its own 50 GiB Windows presets contradict the cap, and the only route is a support request with no published price.</li>
          <li>What a snapshot costs at Cloudflare and at Modal, and what a Cloudflare sandbox costs to start from a snapshot. None of the three is published.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Coverage</h2>
        <p>
          {providers.length} providers and {features.length} features. Every feature page carries a
          row for every provider, including “not published” where the provider is silent.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
